---
scope: src/tools/discover
intent: >
  aide_discover is the map-making tool — the one an agent calls first to see
  where .aide specs live in a project before deciding what to read. It must
  honor progressive disclosure in its own behavior: cheap and shallow when
  asked for the whole project, detailed and content-aware only when asked to
  drill into a specific subtree. The agent uses it as a pointer, not a dump.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A call with no `path` returns a project-wide tree map containing file
      locations and type tags only, with no file content inspected. This is
      the shallow mode — cheap enough to call on every session start without
      hesitation.
    - A call with a `path` returns a detailed subtree of that directory
      only, with summaries drawn from each file and anomaly warnings
      reported alongside the tree. This is the content-aware mode — used
      deliberately when focusing on a specific area.
    - The tool's behavior teaches progressive disclosure by example: agents
      reading the description learn the call-shallow-then-drill pattern and
      carry it into how they approach the rest of the codebase.
    - Output is a single human-readable tree the agent can scan visually,
      with type tags (`[intent]`, `[research]`, `[plan]`, `[todo]`) and inline summaries
      only when summaries exist.
  undesired:
    - A shallow call that reads any file contents. The whole point of the
      shallow mode is being cheap enough to call speculatively; reading
      files silently turns it into the deep mode and burns context for no
      benefit.
    - A shallow call that runs anomaly detection. Anomalies require content
      awareness and are only meaningful when the agent is focused on a
      specific area — surfacing anomalies in the project-wide map either
      floods the output or gives false negatives from missing content.
    - A deep call that walks the entire project instead of just the requested
      subtree. Drill-in is a focusing operation; widening it defeats the
      purpose.
    - Output that dumps summaries even when they're empty strings, producing
      ragged tree lines with trailing " — " artifacts.
---

## Context

discover is where an agent first meets the AIDE convention in practice.
Every other tool is called in response to something discover revealed: read
drills into a spec discover pointed at; scaffold creates into a gap discover
showed; validate acts on anomalies discover only partially reveals. If
discover gets its shape wrong, the whole workflow downstream of it pays the
cost. The discipline of this tool is making its two modes each earn their
runtime cost.

## Strategy

**Two modes, one switch: the presence of `path`.** No path means a
shallow project-wide map — locations and type tags only, no content
inspected. With a path means a deep subtree scan — summaries and anomaly
warnings drawn from the files in that subtree. The switch is not hidden
behind a flag; `path` is the switch, because that's the same field the
agent is already using to scope its attention.

**Shallow is cheap on purpose.** The shallow mode exists so an agent can
call discover at the start of every session without worrying about context
burn. Reading file contents in the shallow mode would turn a cheap,
predictable map into an unpredictable expense and force agents to guess
when it's "safe" to call. The cheapness is the feature.

**Deep is content-aware on purpose.** When the agent has already decided
where to focus, summaries and anomaly warnings earn their cost. The agent
is paying for context because it's about to spend context on that subtree
anyway — summaries let it pick the right spec to read first, and anomaly
warnings surface problems exactly where the agent is about to make
changes.

**Let the tool's own behavior teach the pattern.** An agent reading the
description for discover learns "call with no path for the map, call with
a path to drill in" — and that pattern is progressive disclosure. The
agent internalizes the shape once from discover and then applies it to
the rest of the codebase automatically. This is the only tool in the
server whose *behavior* is part of what it teaches.

**Render the tree for human eyes, not program parsing.** Unicode
box-drawing characters, alphabetical sort, one leaf per line, type tag in
brackets, summary after an em-dash only when present. An empty summary
collapses to "filename [type]" with no trailing punctuation. Agents read
trees well; they tolerate ragged ones poorly.

## Good examples

An agent starts a session in an unfamiliar project and calls `aide_discover`
with no arguments. The response is:

> src/
> ├── service/
> │   ├── notifications/
> │   │   ├── .aide [intent]
> │   │   └── retryPolicy/
> │   │       └── .aide [intent]
> │   └── orders/
> │       ├── research.aide [research]
> │       ├── intent.aide [intent]
> │       └── todo.aide [todo]

Five files, one tree, no summaries, no warnings, no content reads. The
agent now knows the project's spec architecture and decides to drill into
`src/service/orders` next.

The agent then calls `aide_discover` again, this time scoped to the
orders subtree, and gets:

> src/service/orders/
> ├── research.aide [research] — Synthesis of order-completion patterns across common e-commerce platforms
> ├── intent.aide [intent] — Create orders from validated payloads and emit confirmations
> └── todo.aide [todo] — 3 unresolved issues from latest audit run

Same files, but now with summaries pulled from each file's opening line.
The agent has enough context to pick which spec to read first.

## Bad examples

A shallow call that returns this:

> src/
> ├── service/
> │   ├── notifications/
> │   │   ├── .aide [intent] — This module routes notifications through a priority-based queue...

The shallow mode has silently read file contents. It's no longer cheap; it
no longer behaves the way the description promised; and the agent that
called it speculatively just spent context it didn't mean to spend.

A shallow call that reports anomalies for the whole project:

> Warning: naming-conflict at src/service/foo (.aide + intent.aide)
> Warning: orphaned-spec at src/util/bar

The agent called discover for a map and got a health report instead.
Anomaly warnings only belong in the deep subtree mode, where the agent has
asked to focus on that area. Mixing them into a project-wide map dilutes
the map, blurs what the shallow mode is for, and adds tokens the agent
wasn't asking for.

A deep call scoped to the orders subtree that walks the full `src/`
tree anyway, because "the user probably wanted the whole project". Drill-in
is a focusing operation; widening it ignores the agent's explicit scope
request and turns every deep call into the worst of both modes.

A tree where summaries were extracted as empty strings but still printed:

> ├── .aide [intent] —
> ├── intent.aide [intent] —

The trailing em-dashes tell the agent nothing and waste the visual space
that would otherwise help it spot the meaningful entries.
